[{:db/id "q-336222/field-1/07ed32c22c437c6aee2fd2e24913c8d55e86ab4e686556f4b73cddedc67ac8f3" :answer/id #uuid "fbf3043f-851e-5d50-a708-561aa0a33e87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.018-0.01e^{-50t/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228301 :answer-field/correct "q-336222/field-1/07ed32c22c437c6aee2fd2e24913c8d55e86ab4e686556f4b73cddedc67ac8f3" :answer-field/choices ["q-336222/field-1/07ed32c22c437c6aee2fd2e24913c8d55e86ab4e686556f4b73cddedc67ac8f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-336222"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/assets/3a03159ef7285c7cbb571d8870edce98452b197d178334b4b06277f05a38d876.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{125}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{50}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{50}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{3}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{9}{500}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{500}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/3}+\\frac{9}{500}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{125}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{125},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ A{e}^{-50(0)/3}+\\frac{9}{500} & =\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{100}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{100}{e}^{-50t/3}+\\frac{9}{500} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}(9-5{e}^{-50t/3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{1}{100}{e}^{-50t/3}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{9}{500}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-337112/field-1/1f10afbf3d3f57b557bcef9ed72eb87200635ad4d7aac557c751a77759db2a87" :answer/id #uuid "27af37c6-3353-5335-ab27-81718c1bc09f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{35}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337112/field-1/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" :answer/id #uuid "1b1fe0f7-6d4b-5a24-8008-3dda9b5db097" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337112/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "49afba33-7934-5422-aebf-9d750179f1bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-337112/field-1/1f10afbf3d3f57b557bcef9ed72eb87200635ad4d7aac557c751a77759db2a87" :answer-field/choices ["q-337112/field-1/1f10afbf3d3f57b557bcef9ed72eb87200635ad4d7aac557c751a77759db2a87" "q-337112/field-1/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337112" :question/id #uuid "edfe18ab-e7be-583e-8765-080447dfaf9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337112" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+7q=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." :question/worked-solution "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+7{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-7t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+7\\alpha =\\frac{3}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{35}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{35}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-7t}+\\frac{3}{35}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-7t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{3}{35}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{35}$ coulombs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337112/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337112"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337113/field-1/91770ccf162a6a2f9a38c78b34c4dafd213970248b0d982a9e67494fa70b3027" :answer/id #uuid "8c686199-74bd-59f1-b87d-f41dd2b9c99b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{72}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337113/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "348188d0-1a9e-5e59-9a5c-5b4b723bfd00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-337113/field-1/91770ccf162a6a2f9a38c78b34c4dafd213970248b0d982a9e67494fa70b3027" :answer-field/choices ["q-337113/field-1/91770ccf162a6a2f9a38c78b34c4dafd213970248b0d982a9e67494fa70b3027"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337113" :question/id #uuid "f303c54c-d4d4-5d6d-add6-7fe0539476c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-337113" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+9q=\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." :question/worked-solution "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+9{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+9\\alpha =\\frac{5}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{5}{72}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{72}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}+\\frac{5}{72}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-9t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{5}{72}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{5}{72}$ coulombs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337113/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337113"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337122/selection/8290eb6d62125bea63243adec1f2fbebcfb441d7359edd21727a0c82b66cbc64" :answer/id #uuid "1a526002-4349-5c49-a094-5c886a84d9a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.025" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337122/selection/61aa4a2bf1016e8bbcc070e650cc58201cb506d4667d7347d25247acb39f48c9" :answer/id #uuid "5cc2e81d-89d4-5828-b785-6985de1258fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.048" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337122/selection/ee2c5491bd7883adc7b5ba2c5297f4626ae2a0f74f9284ecc8d9de0e94c2cb88" :answer/id #uuid "c8025273-3afb-5f76-85ef-d77104a1b6b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.021" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337122/selection/4afee6b1d6da7a2f38ff80af0d516d120c53bf6d717439bbfdb45be909efe712" :answer/id #uuid "ab15bb63-b856-5e9a-abfe-34bd89a6df41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.029" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337122/selection/30d95447e1056d0d672c6ebfe769e2541865a02f1547b16c9fe955940a6310b6" :answer/id #uuid "da0761db-ec87-5b97-b0cd-ca1ae447df99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.067" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337122/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cda45fd6-b384-5df8-9349-0b2426c23742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-337122/selection/8290eb6d62125bea63243adec1f2fbebcfb441d7359edd21727a0c82b66cbc64" :answer-field/choices ["q-337122/selection/8290eb6d62125bea63243adec1f2fbebcfb441d7359edd21727a0c82b66cbc64" "q-337122/selection/61aa4a2bf1016e8bbcc070e650cc58201cb506d4667d7347d25247acb39f48c9" "q-337122/selection/ee2c5491bd7883adc7b5ba2c5297f4626ae2a0f74f9284ecc8d9de0e94c2cb88" "q-337122/selection/4afee6b1d6da7a2f38ff80af0d516d120c53bf6d717439bbfdb45be909efe712" "q-337122/selection/30d95447e1056d0d672c6ebfe769e2541865a02f1547b16c9fe955940a6310b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337122" :question/id #uuid "98591216-f2fb-53d6-90e1-e780d730c534" :question/math-academy-id "q-337122" :question/problem "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.004\\,\\text{F}.$ Find, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.06$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{100}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}q=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{25}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{6}{125}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{125}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3}+\\frac{6}{125}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{100},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{100} \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)/3}+\\frac{6}{125} & =\\frac{1}{100} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{19}{500}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{6}{125}-\\frac{19}{500}{e}^{-25t/3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.06$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.06)=\\frac{6}{125}-\\frac{19}{500}{e}^{-25(0.06)/3}≈0.025,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-337122/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337122"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337135/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "34c9380f-50eb-563c-b279-3d4984070258" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337135/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "b54692c3-22cc-59ab-b2ca-5f620d931518" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337135/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "85fb63d4-5445-5d63-ab45-9c225861c63a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337135/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "973f931b-2b4b-54eb-b405-b5394da0d730" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337135/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "44837f77-aa0e-5dfa-a922-cde2033f5ad3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337135/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "25388900-7637-5209-9190-49f8a3fbe4ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-337135/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-337135/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-337135/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-337135/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-337135/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-337135/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337135" :question/id #uuid "23410692-ee8d-52af-9eed-f0c04ce14d9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337135" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/assets/6c5ce500d937822081d819e2c02f5fabece926498dd196e65d64dbdaa1dcb0bc.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{P}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{Q}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +3\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{4}{125}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{3}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{4}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{4}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=4,Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=4+3=7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337135/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337135"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
